WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.320
Colin Farrell's transformation into the Penguin
caught everyone by surprise – even his co-stars

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:06.450
in "The Batman."

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.220
He spent hours in the make-up chair, and so
much more.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.490
Here's how he made that intense transformation.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:14.999
If you went into "The Batman" without knowing
anything about the film, you'd probably be

00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:17.930
surprised to learn that Colin Farrell is actually
in it.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.970
In "The Batman," Farrell plays just one of
multiple antagonists going up against a Batman

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.369
who's only been on the job for two years.

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:27.760
That's when a mysterious figure called The
Riddler begins leaving dead bodies around

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.320
town with messages for the caped crusader.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.280
Farrell's depiction of Oswald Cobblepot, or
the Penguin, is already earning praise from

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.210
critics and fans alike, and Warner Bros. has
thrown their support behind the performance

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:41.810
by announcing a Penguin-centered spin-off
series for HBO Max.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:44.590
"Whoa, take it easy sweetheart."

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:48.400
That seems like a sure sign that Farrell's
Penguin role is only getting bigger from here

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.400
on out.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.600
But what did it take for Farrell to make such
a striking transformation?

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.980
A whole lot, it turns out.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:58.430
In an interview with Total Film, Colin Farrell
said that there was always going to be more

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:02.160
to his portrayal of the character than the
sheer physicality, although it took finally

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.409
seeing the full prosthetic get-up to hammer
home who the Penguin was.

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:06.409
He said:

00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:10.670
"I knew where I was going with that, but I
wasn't fully comfortable until I saw the makeup.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.479
When I saw the makeup, it just all became
really, really clear."

00:01:13.479 --> 00:01:18.420
Before that, Farrell's preparation had mostly
involved finding the psychology of the Penguin.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.900
In "The Batman," Cobblepot isn't as high on
the criminal hierarchy as he's often depicted

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:23.900
in the comics.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.310
Farrell explained to Total Film that he really
tried to examine where the character is during

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:31.079
the movie, and the journey to full-fledged
Batman villain that he still has ahead of

00:01:31.079 --> 00:01:32.079
him.

00:01:32.079 --> 00:01:35.740
"Every discussion until I saw what Mike Marino,
the makeup artist, had designed, was considering

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.810
the character's psychology and where he was
at this stage of his criminal career."

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.840
The makeup and prosthetics involved proved
transformational on such a level that even

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.640
most die-hard Farrell fans barely recognized
him when the first trailer for "The Batman"

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.920
was released.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.320
Farrell has publicly praised the work of these
artists for helping define the character so

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.460
clearly – for audiences as well as for himself
as an actor.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.140
"You know, I could see the history on this
man's face.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.490
I could see what a hard life he'd had.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:05.240
And just behavior and mannerisms just were
instantly informed."

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.080
Under such heavy prosthetics and makeup, it
should come as no surprise that Colin Farrell's

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:13.190
Penguin took hours to prepare — despite
the on-screen character having a relatively

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:16.180
simple costume compared to everyone else in
the movie.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:17.590
Farrell explained to Total Film:

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:19.860
"Someone was there that had molded the teeth.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.770
Someone was there that had done the hair.

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:25.330
Mike and his team were there, three or four
of them were applying different pieces because

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.970
it's six or eight different individual prosthetic
pieces.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:32.970
And once it all went on, honest to God, it
overtook me, as I think it would most people."

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:36.220
Another drastic change to Colin Farrell's
look is his size.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:39.890
The Irish-born actor has bulked up for films
like "Total Recall," but he put on the extra

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.430
pounds in a very different way for "The Batman."

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.810
The weight, however, was a happy accident.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:48.260
Farrell had just filmed the miniseries "The
North Water," a role for which he'd intentionally

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.500
packed on some extra pounds.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.340
When he met with Matt Reeves for "The Batman,"
Reeves loved the look.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.270
As Farrell described the meeting to Total
Film:

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.140
"Matt was like, 'Oh, yeah, I love it.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:58.150
I think that's Oz!'

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:02.170
And I was like, 'Nah, man, let's have a thin
Oz because I'm not feeling great now, I have

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:03.370
to lose this weight.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.480
There's no way I was keeping it on, man.'"

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.060
In the end, they compromised: Colin Farrell
got to lose the extra weight, while Reeves

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:13.230
got his Oz through pounds of prosthetics that
took four hours to apply before each shooting

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:14.230
day.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:17.840
The end result of the transformation was so
convincing that even some of his costars failed

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:18.920
to recognize him.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:24.290
"Jeffrey Wright walked by me and I said, 'Morning,
man,' and he looked at me like he wanted me

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:26.100
thrown off the set."

